// ============================================================================
// DAILY — 2026-10-06 (Tuesday) — Carnatic melakarta math
// Melakarta #65: Mechakalyani (Kalyani)
// ============================================================================
//
// THE NUMBERING SYSTEM (chakra/position scheme, same real combinatorial
// structure used every Tuesday this project has run — 08-25's #22, 09-01's
// #20, 09-08's #57, 09-15's #29, 09-22's #23, 09-29's #21):
//   chakra   = ceil(N / 6),  position = N - 6*(chakra - 1)
// For N = 65: chakra = ceil(65/6) = 11 -> chakras 7-12 mirror chakras 1-6's
//             Ri-Ga combo pattern (N > 36 means prati Ma), base index =
//             11 - 6 = 5 -> Ri-Ga combo #5 of the 6-combo cycle (1=R1G1
//             2=R1G2 3=R1G3 4=R2G2 5=R2G3 6=R3G3) = R2 G3 (chatushruti Ri,
//             antara Ga)
//             position = 65 - 6*10 = 5 -> Dha-Ni combo #5 of its own 6-combo
//             cycle (1=D1N1 2=D1N2 3=D1N3 4=D2N2 5=D2N3 6=D3N3) = D2 N3
//             (chatushruti Dha, kakali Ni)
//             N > 36 -> prati Ma (M2), not shuddha
// melakarta 65 = S R2 G3 M2 P D2 N3. Semitones (S=0 R1=1 R2/G1=2 R3/G2=3
// G3=4 M1=5 M2=6 P=7 D1=8 D2/N1=9 D3/N2=10 N3=11): 0 2 4 6 7 9 11 — checked,
// not assumed: this is EXACTLY tonaljs's "lydian" mode. That's genuinely
// interesting, not just a coincidence of naming: it's the SAME interval set
// as Hindustani Kalyan thaat (daily/2026-08-24-hindustani-kalyan.strudel,
// also D:lydian) — two independent systems (Hindustani parent-scale theory,
// Carnatic melakarta combinatorics) landing on the identical scale, and even
// the names echo each other (Kalyan / Kalyani, both from "auspicious"). Tonic
// kept at D specifically to make that convergence audible, not just
// theoretical — the same pitch center as 08-24, deliberately, not a repeat.
//   -> .scale("D:lydian")
//
// THE DEFINING FEATURE: the raised 4th (M2, prati Madhyama, degree 3) is a
// tritone above the tonic — the only such interval in melakarta's natural
// scale degrees, and the source of Lydian's characteristic open, floating,
// unresolved brightness. Every other Tuesday so far has been a minor-family
// or major-with-no-tension color (20 aeolian, 21 harmonic minor, 23 melodic
// minor, 29 plain major, 57 double-augmented-second hungarian minor) — #65
// is the first BRIGHT, lydian-colored Tuesday, and the thing to keep audible
// is that tritone, not just the major-ish surface.
//
// ARRANGEMENT — built around making the S->M# tritone a continuous presence,
// not just a melodic passing event:
//   drone: sine (purest waveform, MUSIC-KNOWLEDGE.md §2.5), but tonic + the
//          RAISED 4TH (d2/g#2) instead of the usual tanpura tonic+fifth every
//          prior Tuesday has used — the fifth is generic to nearly every
//          scale and wouldn't say anything about THIS melakarta specifically;
//          droning the tritone itself keeps the raga's one defining tension
//          sounding underneath the whole pattern, the way 10-05's Marwa drone
//          retuned its second string to Re instead of Pa for the same reason
//          (voice the interval that actually makes the raga the raga).
//   lead:  supersaw (harmonically dense, MUSIC-KNOWLEDGE.md §2.5's "plucked"
//          guidance) — reached for again after 09-29, but for a different
//          reason this time: there, it gave an augmented second more bite;
//          here, the dense overtone stack makes the tritone against the
//          drone shimmer rather than clash, which is the Lydian-specific
//          effect wanted (brightness, not edge). Fast attack/short decay for
//          a plucked attack, generous room for the "floating" quality.
//   NO DRUM KIT — same standing call as every melakarta exposition so far
//   (MUSIC-QC-LOOP.md's autopilot-percussion lesson): this is a scale study
//   over a drone (alapana-style), not a tala-bound kriti, so no metric pulse
//   is implied. The two derived numbers still drive real audible things:
//   chakra (11) sets the lead filter LFO's sweep period, position (5) sets
//   the delay time (a quintuplet subdivision) — neither needed a drum layer
//   as its "audible outlet."
//
// MELODY — four phrases (call / climax-response / drop / retrograde-flavored
// variation), anchored on S and P (the two swaras every melakarta shares),
// all built to keep the S->M# tritone (scale degrees 0->3) audible from
// multiple directions, not just once at a cadence:
//   A (call): a leap straight from the tonic into the tritone, held through
//     a rest (the "floating," ungrounded quality — not resolving yet), then
//     a further climb to the fifth before cutting off:
//                                          0 ~ 3 ~ ~ 4 ~ ~
//   B (climax/response, call-and-response device): from above the octave, a
//     leap down through the octave itself into the SAME tritone approached
//     from the other side (octave to raised-4th is also a tritone — the
//     interval is symmetric around the octave), then a stepwise fall to the
//     second degree — the "response" retraces the tritone descending:
//                                          9 ~ 7 ~ 3 2 1 ~
//   C (the drop, tension->release device): a big leap from the octave down
//     through the tritone again, then resolves through the leading tone
//     approached from BELOW (degree -1) up into the tonic — tension built
//     across A and B finally released onto S:
//                                          7 ~ 3 ~ 1 ~ -1 0
//   D (motif-and-variation of A — same tritone emphasis, inverted contour:
//     a stepwise descent from the fifth through the tritone this time
//     approached from above, down to the tonic, closing with a grace-leap up
//     to the second before settling):
//                                          4 3 ~ 2 1 ~ 0 ~
//
// CYCLES = 24 (65 itself is far too many cycles for a 15-25s clip — same
// adjustment 09-08's #57 made). setcpm(72) -> 24 * 60/72 = 20.0s exactly, six
// clean passes of the 4-phrase rotation.
// ============================================================================

setcpm(72)
let CYCLES = 24 // 24 * 60/72 = 20.0s; six clean passes of the 4-phrase melody

stack(
  // DRONE — tonic + RAISED 4TH (not the usual tonic+fifth, see ARRANGEMENT
  // above), so the raga's one defining tension sounds continuously, not just
  // in the melody. Plain sine, slow attack/release so it swells rather than
  // snaps on.
  note("<d2 g#2>/4")
    .sound("sine")
    .gain(.26)
    .attack(2.2).release(3.2)
    .room(4.5),

  // MELODY — see MELODY note above. Real leaps (a tritone from multiple
  // directions, thirds, a leap from below the tonic), rests for breathing
  // room, call-and-response (A/B), tension->release (C), and motif-and-
  // variation (D) as the required jazz-improv devices.
  n(`<
    0 ~ 3 ~ ~ 4 ~ ~
    9 ~ 7 ~ 3 2 1 ~
    7 ~ 3 ~ 1 ~ -1 0
    4 3 ~ 2 1 ~ 0 ~
  >`)
    .scale("D:lydian")
    .sound("supersaw")
    .attack(.008).decay(.12).sustain(.18).release(.15)
    .lpf(sine.range(700, 3400).slow(11)) // chakra (11) -> sweep period
    .lpq(7)
    .sometimes(x => x.ply(2)) // improv hook: occasional stuttered ornament
    .gain(.42)
    .room(2.6)
    .delay(.22)
    .delaytime(1/5) // position (5) -> a quintuplet delay
    .delayfeedback(.3)
)
